WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.472
This example combines WHERE, GROUP BY, and HAVING in one query.

00:00:04.472 --> 00:00:09.257
The row-level rule keeps sales whose price is at least twenty-five dollars.

00:00:09.257 --> 00:00:12.942
Those qualifying rows are then grouped by category.

00:00:12.942 --> 00:00:17.167
We calculate total quantity and total revenue for each category.

00:00:17.167 --> 00:00:21.467
Finally, HAVING keeps only groups whose total quantity is above the required threshold.

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:26.752
Before running it, predict whether every category will remain.

00:00:26.752 --> 00:00:30.582
Then run the query and compare the result.

00:00:30.582 --> 00:00:34.952
Look carefully at the difference between the price condition and the quantity condition.

00:00:34.952 --> 00:00:38.852
Price belongs to each source row, so it is checked in WHERE.

00:00:38.852 --> 00:00:44.562
Total quantity exists only after grouping, so it is checked in HAVING.

00:00:44.562 --> 00:00:50.552
This is the full aggregation pattern you will use frequently in real analytical work.

00:00:50.552 --> 00:00:55.177
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:55.177 --> 00:01:01.117
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:01:01.117 --> 00:01:03.712
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:03.712 --> 00:01:09.007
In a real setting such as a dashboard summarizing admissions by department and month,

00:01:09.007 --> 00:01:15.147
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:15.147 --> 00:01:17.272
before discussing syntax.

00:01:17.272 --> 00:01:23.016
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
